module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.absolute_extra_source_root_panics_instead_of_typed_refusal

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BlockerClassUnstated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data absolute_extra_source_root_panics_instead_of_typed_refusal: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "absolute_extra_source_root_panics_instead_of_typed_refusal" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "**an absolute extra --source-root panics in repo_relative_path_normalized instead of refusing with a typed cause** (INVALID STATE: `module_index_path_key` documents that files under an out-of-tree absolute root have no workspace-relative spelling and that the absolute path IS the canonical key. A caller that reaches `repo_relative_path_normalized` on that same absolute directory -- `pool_roots_for_module_graph_closure` does, for every absolute `--source-root` -- panics: `path /tmp/ann_priv is not under process workspace root`. DESIGN section 5: a panic is below the floor; the arm is a typed located refusal.",

    "HARM: the only consumers that pass an absolute extra root are composed-root and fixture trees. The crash looks like a host or runner defect. Nobody rediscovers it behind a flag combination the public floor never uses.",

    "RECEIPT (2026-09-10): public SHA 0d6b6653e0f, `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --source-root /tmp/ann_priv --entry /tmp/ann_priv/probe.dag --function probe_main` panics at `src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs` `repo_relative_path_normalized` with that path. The same bytes under a workspace-relative extra root `_ann_probe` reach ordinary run (ProcessExit wrapping of a Bool, not annotation refusal).",

    "It is not `extra_source_root_outside_workspace_refuses_annotations_as_unattached`: that class completes resolve and lied with UnattachedAtScopeEnd (a refusal parse-comment-gap-attachment deleted; that row records its standing since). This class never reaches attachment. A repair that types the panic does not by itself attach annotations, and a repair that attaches annotations under a relative extra root does not stop the absolute-path panic.",

    "SECOND SPELLING, SAME WRAPPER (calm-crane-178, 2026-10-16): the panic is not confined to `--source-root`. `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry <absolute path> --function __no_such_function__` panics in `repo_relative_path_normalized` too, when run from a checkout whose absolute entry path is not workspace-relative. The same invocation with a RELATIVE `--entry` completes resolve and refuses with its real diagnostics. It first read as a git-worktree defect and was misattributed to one; the discriminator is absolute versus relative, not worktree versus plain checkout. The ceiling and next-rung trigger below name both spellings. The row id says `extra_source_root` and undernames the class; it is left unrenamed to keep the id stable for existing citations.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: below the ladder -- process death, no typed cause.",

    "CEILING: structurally guaranteed once every path reaching `repo_relative_path_normalized` -- an extra source root that is an existing directory, and an `--entry` path, absolute or not -- is keyed by `module_index_path_key` and never by panicking `repo_relative_path_normalized`.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: any absolute path that names an existing directory (an extra `--source-root`) or an entry file (`--entry`) is admitted into the module index under its absolute key, or refused with a located typed diagnostic naming that path -- sufficient for BOTH `gunbc run --source-root /tmp/overlay` and `gunbc run --entry <absolute path>` to not panic; fixing one spelling does not retire this row. `try_repo_relative_path_normalized` already returns None; the defect is the panicking wrapper on a path `module_index_path_key` was written to accept.)",

    "SECOND WALK, SAME WRAPPER DEFECT, FOUND BY THE v1 MODULE-INDEX ADMISSION INVENTORY (lane K, wave 2 of the v1 compile-performance plan, 2026-10-19; inventory only -- fix belongs to lane P / P1.2). INVALID STATE: the seed's module-binding walk (`for_each_parsed_module_binding`, src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs) dies by its own `panic!` on two observation failures, both of which ALREADY HAVE TYPED RESULT ARMS: `collect_dag_files` calls `collect_dag_files_result(...).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!)` on the error's rendering on `read_dir` failure, and per file `source.read().unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!)` with a message naming the path and the error on read failure. The walk's own doc comment promises what only the PARSE arm delivers: \"Parse failures do not panic and are not skipped: each becomes a typed located `ModuleBindingRefusal`\" -- the parse arm was written that way and the read arms never were, so the file whose bytes could not be obtained and the directory whose listing could not be taken die in panic form below the DESIGN section 5 floor while their siblings in the same population complete. HARM: same shape as this row's opening specimen -- a crash that reads like a host or runner defect; here it additionally breaks the walk's own stated contract, so a reviewer reading the comment believes the population is refused-typed. DISTINGUISHING FACT from this row's first two receipts: not path spelling -- an existing file rendered unreadable (permission, transient I/O, a file deleted between listing and read) panics under wholly relative roots; the discriminator is observation failure versus parse failure, one arm typed, its neighbors untyped. Not `unreadable_source_omitted_from_dependency_population` either: that class SILENTLY OMITS, which is the softer failure; this one stops the run, and the two are the two halves of the same obligation done wrong in opposite directions -- the repair neither swallows nor kills. RUNG FOUND AT: below the ladder -- process death where the typed arm exists. CEILING: 3, structurally guaranteed -- both sites already return `Result`; refuse as `ModuleBindingRefusal` siblings (cause + path) are produced today for parse, at zero extra reading. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a walked directory whose `read_dir` fails, or a listed `.dag` whose read fails, refuses with a typed located diagnostic naming the path and nonzero exit beside any parse refusals already collected -- sufficient that neither spellings' panic strings appear in stderr; not satisfied by catching the panic at a higher frame, which keeps the run dead.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
